Confirmed: even with an aggressive suicide bot (holding up + oscillating x, ramming into everything), it takes about 300+ seconds of simulation (5 minutes or more, and these runs hit the 5-minute cap while still on wave 3 — wait, "died at 300s (wave 3)" — my message logic: state changed from playing, so it output 'died at'… no wait: while the loop condition is `state==='playing'`, the cap is 300 seconds. Outputting "died at 300s sim (wave 3)" means the loop terminated at the 300-second cap — but state should be 'playing'… my message is ambiguous: I output "died" when the loop terminates, but the loop also terminates at the cap. Let me double-check: `while(game.state==='playing' && f<50*60*5)` — it terminates either by death or by the cap. The message says "died at X or still alive at 5min" — I can't distinguish them! At 300 seconds all runs terminated. All at exactly 300 seconds = cap → still alive (state playing). Ugh, my sloppy message.

So: even the aggressive suicide bot survived the full 5-minute cap (it got to wave 3 and was still alive when I stopped it). Current tuning: under sustained ramming, the player doesn't die in 5 minutes. M3's "player can die" fails against any realistic test budget.

Need a fix. Going with the plan: in training mode, collision damage bypasses i-frames, 1 damage per 15 frames of overlap. Faithful mode unchanged (original per-frame damage).

Let's implement it. Collision branch in enemyThink (around line ~1190):
```js
this.subEnergy((suben >> 2) >> (this.training ? 1 : 0));
```
→
```js
if (this.training) {
  // Training: sustained contact bleeds 1 shield / 15 frames, unaffected by i-frames
  if ((this._frame % 15) === 0) this.subEnergy(1, true);
} else {
  this.subEnergy(suben >> 2);
}
```
Wait — but the original collision also deals damage to the enemy every frame (sprite.hits -= PLAYERWIDTH/2) — keep that. And the original plays the FX_CRASH sound every frame during overlap (SND_Patch FX_CRASH) — check what my port does there (I saw startAnim A_SMALL_AIR_EXPLO around line 1193). Let me read the collision block.

Also, subEnergy needs a bypassInv parameter.

One fine point: subEnergy(1, true) with DIFF_0 halving: amt=1, not >1 → stays at 1 ✓. And when bypassing, _inv is not set — good (a collision shouldn't grant i-frames from enemy fire… or should it? If a collision sets i-frames, enemy shots get blocked more → the bot survives longer. Don't set _inv on a bypass collision: add `if (this.training && !bypassInv) this._inv = this.trainingInv;`… actually simpler: only set _inv when !bypassInv.

Next, measure the death time: expected value about 100 shield / (1/15f*50fps = 3.33 dps from collision + about 1–2 dps from shots) ≈ 20–30 seconds of continuous contact. The suicide bot's contact isn't 100% continuous, but it should die well within 60 seconds, probably during wave 1.

Then re-run the M5 battery.

